      "writtenOn": "2026-10-15T17:00:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This is a genuine requirement for IBM systems, where certain types of dumps can be quite large in size (often exceeding 500 MB). Once support for this functionality is available, we will have a corresponding client implementation in place.\n\nSorry, why does a large size require range support?  Range support is for resuming downloads.  Large downloads can be streamed without having to have impact on the client.\n\n\u003e \n\u003e For testing purposes, I developed a client that retrieves the dump in multiple 1 MB chunks. After all the chunks are downloaded, the client stitches them together to reconstruct the complete file. I have validated this approach, and it works as expected.\n\nThis is part of the issue.  \"I developed a client\" doesn\u0027t mean there\u0027s any support for this in the Redfish ecosystem.  That\u0027s not to say I want to prevent it, I just want to make sure we have SOME test coverage in one of our tools so we don\u0027t regress this.",
      "parentUuid": "33092263_2ac8d417",
      "revId": "a2121ff037fc22eec20e68e58a209d7df0930f4c",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4fe7e238_7fa985eb",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001762
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-16T06:00:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why should we dictate how clients use range support? Once the capability is available, it\u0027s up to the client to decide how best to leverage it. For example, a client could request the first 1 MB, then the next 1 MB, and so on. These requests can even be issued in parallel and stitched together once all chunks are received.\n\nThe retry behavior you mentioned would apply to individual chunks as well. From the server\u0027s perspective, however, nothing changes. The server simply serves the requested ranges and does not need to be aware of how the client chooses to consume them.\n\n We won\u0027t have any client-side usage at this point because there is no server-side implementation yet. If you\u0027re referring to unit test coverage for the feature, then I\u0027m completely fine with adding those test cases.",
      "parentUuid": "cba81c17_e0b96dd8",
      "revId": "a2121ff037fc22eec20e68e58a209d7df0930f4c",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    }
